This contract tests the set (assuming the given index is valid) functionality of the ArrayList with an array list instance, an item x, and an index.

The 'Old' variables hold the initial states of the list and the 'New' variables hold the final states of the list after the operation.

After the operation, the test checks the following:

- The count of the list before and after the operation remain the same.
- The index of x before and after the operation are the same.
- The last index of x before and after the operation are the same.
- Whether x is contained in the list before and after the operation are the same.

The test also makes use of PexObserve to help observe the states. 

Finally, we have a Debug Assertion to ensure the index of the new element after the set is greater than 0 (if it is, there is no problem).



A: From what I can understand from your code and the examples given, you're using Path Testing Extension (PTE) to test your custom ArrayList class. This extension allows you to do a more detailed testing with various combinations of your class' methods.

Now in your PUT_SetContract method, it appears the assumption is that the index you pass in is valid (doesn't throw an exception) and that the index you pass in is within the bounds of the array (it's a number from 0 to Count - 1).

Here is the contract you want to test:

1) When I try to PUT (Set) an element in the ArrayList at a specified index.

2) Then, if the index is valid, the ArrayList count should remain the same.

3) And if the element did not exist at the index before, its index should be the same as the index where you just PUT (Set) the new element.

4) And if an element is replaced at the index, the index of the new element should be the same as the index where you just PUT the new element.

5) Also, the index of the replaced element should not change.

This could be accomplished by writing test cases for each of the conditions above.

Here is an example for a test case written by Pex for the Set function:

[PexMethod(@"System.Collections.ArrayList.Set(int,System.Object)")]
public void TestSet([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList list, int index, object value)
{
    // store the initial state
    int initialCount = list.Count;
    object preValue = index < list.Count ? list[index] : null;
    
    // execute the operation
    list.Set(index, value);

    // check the post conditions
    if(index < list.Count) {
        Assert.AreEqual(value, list[index]);
    }
    if(preValue != null) {
        Assert.AreEqual(initialCount, list.Count);
        Assert.AreEqual(index, list.IndexOf(preValue));
    } else {
        Assert.AreEqual(initialCount, list.Count);
        Assert.AreEqual(index, list.IndexOf(value));
    }
}

This is a simple example of how you could test set function with PTE. Please modify it based on your need in your specific custom ArrayList class you have. 

In the contract, an assumption (specification) is being made that the index is within the range of the list (non-negative and less than Count). 

The observe is like a console.log for seeing the variables in the debug output, you can use it to debug your test cases.

Finally it performs a Debug Assertion to ensure the index of the new element after the set is greater than 0. This test is trying to ensure that the new element is actually added to the list rather than overwritten.

Note: This is just a small excerpt, the actual contract code should be complete. As you mentioned, Pex is powerful tool and you should definitely explore the features and possibilities it offers for your specific ArrayList class.
Hope this helps you.

