A: You should separate your tests into two parts - those that check for the desired behavior and those that check for the functionality of your ArrayList. The behavior tests should test your ArrayList in a way that mimics how the ArrayList is used in a real-world application. 
The functionality tests should test your ArrayList in a way that mimics how the ArrayList is implemented.
This is important because the desired behavior tests should check if your code is producing the correct results according to the contract and the functionality tests should check that the ArrayList behaves correctly according to its implementation.
For example,


*

*For testing your ArrayList's Set method, you would want to test how it changes the value at a specific index, but you wouldn't want to test how it changes the value at a random index. Instead, you would want to test it by setting the value of the first element, the middle element, and the last element.

*You would want to test how it handles null values when an element is inserted, and you would want to test what happens when you try to Set a value to an index that doesn't exist.


For the same reason, you wouldn't want to test your ArrayList's IndexOf method with a random value. Instead, you would want to test it with values that exist in the ArrayList and values that don't.
This way, your tests are more likely to catch bugs in the behaviors and functionality of your ArrayList.
Hope it will help you to avoid the situation you have encountered.
Regards.

A: If you want to test the setting of values and observing changes, you should test your case with a different, non-null value and check if the count has maintained. 
For example:
1. Set an initial count to some value say 5.
2. Perform the Set operation and count should be maintain.
3. Perform again the Set operation and count should be maintain.

The advantage here is you have a base case to compare with, which you should always have with any collection. 
Hope that helps.

A: In addition to other people's answers:


*

*For methods changing the size of the collection (like Add or Remove), you should test how the count changes. Testing behavior is not enough to ensure that method behaves correctly when it should increments/decrements the count.

*Also, for properties (like Count) you should ensure that they return correct value after manipulation of collection. 

*Make sure to test cases when you are trying to access out of range index or trying to set null or value types in non-generic list.

*Write tests with edge element access i.e., 0-th index and last index of element in the list.

*You could also try to add some order to the testing. Like add elements in specific order and then try to access them in reverse order (not always applicable for all collections but can be useful for testing).


Please remember that testing should not be seen as a task to only cover all possible scenarios, but provide value to the developer. If a method works correctly for these cases, you shouldn't add more scenarios just to test it, but rather, ensure that your requirements are handled correctly.

A: Answering your question from the title:

How can I write Pex tests for this method?

Generally, you would use Parameterized Testing Extensibility Framework (Pex) to write tests that cover different parameters for your scenario. In your situation, this would be ArrayList parameters and a non-negative index. 

Here's what a test would look like for setting an item in the list using Pex:

```csharp
[PexMethod]
public void ArrayList_SetItem_Test(
   [PexAssumeValid] ArrayList list,
   [PexValidateNotNull] object item,
   [PexRange(0, int.MaxValue)] int index)
{ 
   Assume.True(index <= list.Count); // The index needs to be within range.

   list.Set(index, item);

   Assert.AreEqual(item, list[index]); // If we made it this far, the operation worked.
}
```

In this scenario, we are testing whether--------------------            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);
            
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x after setting is more than or same as index
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= index);
            
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ count after setting is same as old count
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);
            
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ x after setting is in the list
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX);
            
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ index of x after setting is same as index
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == index || New_arrListLastIndexOfX == index);

        }

    }
}
```

You can test the PexTest using PexRunner. Below is a simple snippet of how you can use it.

```
PexRunner.DefaultOptions =
    new Microsoft.Pex.Framework.PexOptions(
        PexTestAttribute.DefaultTimeOut,                     // Time out (15 seconds).
        false,                                             // Parameterized tests.
        100,                                               // Maximum input test.
        100,                                               // Maximum input counts.
        100,                                               // Maximum input size for each element (100 chars).
        10,                                                // Maximum random input test.
        5,                                                 // Random seed.
        false,                                             // Generate and execute pairwise unit tests.
        0                                                  // Random range start.
    );
```

You can see that we have given the options for our PexTest such as Timeout, Parameterized tests, Maximum input counts and so on. According to requirements, these options can be changed.

Let me know if you have any further comments or doubts.


*

*Note: For running PEX tests, you need to install PexTestRunner NuGet Package and add references to Microsoft.Pex.Framework and Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Rules namespace in your test project.

Hope these guides help you understand the testing concepts and how to use PEX for testing array lists in .NET framework. Let me know in case you have any more questions.

A: The contract for the ArrayList.Set method is:

public virtual void Set(int index, object value)

where:

• index - is the zero-based index of the element to set.

• value - is the new value for the element at the specified index.


The Set method changes the value at the specified index in the ArrayList.

The behavior is undefined if index is less than 0 or greater than or equal to Count.

This method does not raise the CountChanged event. The method does not throw OutOfMemoryException if the underlying implementation of ArrayList has reallocated the storage.

There is a difference between the ArrayList.Insert and ArrayList.Set methods. The ArrayList.Insert method inserts an item at the specified index and shifts the other items to the right, whereas the ArrayList.Set method replaces the specified index with a new item and does not affect the items to the right of the specified index.

The ArrayList.Set method does not preserve the order of the elements.

Please note you can't set an ArrayList to null, throw exception or have duplicate item in an ArrayList.

Also, ArrayList.Set method can throw ArgumentOutOfRangeException if the index is less than 0 or greater than Count, and InvalidCastException if the type of the specified object cannot be cast to the type of the element in the ArrayList. 

Please remember that in an ArrayList, duplicate items are allowed and null is also allowed in an ArrayList.

To test Set in ArrayList use Pex. Here is an example:
public void PexTestMethod([PexMethodUnderTest] ArrayList al)
{
    int IndexToInsert = 2;
    DateTime DateAdded = DateTime.Now;
    al.Set(IndexToInsert, DateAdded);
}

The code above tests the Set method by inserting a DateTime object at a particular index in the ArrayList. The method Set will automatically cast the DateTime object to object type because it's the most general type.

Please replace index of the element and value at that index accordingly.

