This is an ArrayList contract test with a `PUT_SetContract` test method to verify the behavior of `ArrayList.set` operation.

I used the following parameters and assumptions:

1. `arrList`: The input arrayList to be tested.
2. `x`: This is the value that will be set to an index of the arrayList.
3. `index`: This is the index where the value x will be set.

An assumption has been made that the index should be within the range of 0 and arrayList count. Also, the values for index of x before and after the operation are observed. 

To make the test robust, it performs the following checks:

1. Checks if count of arrayList doesn't change after set operation.
2. Checks if index of x after set operation is same as input index.
3. Checks if last index of x after set operation is same as input index.
4. Checks if x is in the arrayList after set operation.
5. Checks if value at old index is still x.

These assertions are all unit tests generated by Pex and observe the behavior of the `set` operation on ArrayList. I think Pex is a great tool to automatically generate such tests for your methods which are hard to reach or test manually. 

In this method, I provided a unique test name and tried to provide a meaningful message by using PexObserve and Debug.Assert, It might not be 100% applicable in other scenarios, but I have tried to make it more comprehensible and meaningful for such a scenario.

Please note that the test might not pass or fail based on the actual behavior of the `set` operation. You might need to modify the Assertions (or PexObserve lines in case you wish to check the values of some variables) based on the expected actual behavior. 

Also, please note that Pex requires the tests to be independent from each other and should not rely on any state outside of the method being tested. It doesn't support shared/static state.

This contract test could be considered as a very fundamental test which can be expanded upon to test more of the ArrayList behavior (e.g., InsertRange, RemoveRange, Clear, etc.)

Remember, each test in Pex should be able to run independently, without any side-effects in the other tests.


Refer Pex's Test-Driven Development guide in MSDN for more insights.

Thanks for reading. It's always good to know different testing frameworks and approaches. It will help your tool-selection in the future.

For more details, see the post - http://srikanthradarsam.com/2014/03/05/pex-testing-framework-and-nunit-introduction/#sm.01vx4krq09jf0zhjvhk

Hope you find this helpful.

--Srikanth 









